#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175617 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "70569afd-42c4-5e5f-8397-4fbd873eb8e4" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11002" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\cos x$? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n1. The range of the function is $[-1,1]$.\n2. The function has minimum values at $x =n\\pi$.\n3. The function intersects the $x$-axis at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} +\\pi n$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos{x}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3557/Source/Images/57d6cfd2524b25259c227c929e2b4545.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The minimum value is $-1$, and the maximum value is $1$. Therefore, the range is $[-1,1]$.\n- Statement II is false. The function has a minimum at $x=\\pi$ and the period is $2\\pi$. Therefore, the minimum values occur at $\\pi+2n\\pi$.\n- Statement III is true. These are the zeros of the cosine curve. The cosine curve has zeros at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175618 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c25c3a0-083a-55d5-b688-7347bfafaa9e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a8cc8b8f-463b-5734-a33d-e915159cba32" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11003" :question/problem "If $\\cos x =-\\dfrac {1}{3}$, find the value of $2\\cos(x + 4\\pi)+3$." :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\cos x$ is $2\\pi$. Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\cos{(x+2n\\pi)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos x &= \\cos (x + 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot π) \\\\\n&= \\cos (x + 4π)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} =-\\dfrac {1}{3}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\cos{(x+4\\pi)} =-\\dfrac {1}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos (x + 4π) + 3 &= 2(-\\frac{1}{3}) + 3 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3} + 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175627 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94645507-186a-54a7-a96b-ccfb0fb1f769" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "08253dde-510a-5451-ba5c-64d42d647db2" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11012" :question/problem "If $\\cos x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}$, then find the value of $\\cos (-x-6\\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\cos{x}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3557/Source/Images/dfab92fbee88df2003b54abc658b5d81.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\cos{x}$ is $2\\pi$.\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\cos{x}$ is an *even* function, and we have\n$\\cos(-x) = \\cos{x}$.\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\cos{x}$, we have\n$\\cos (-x - 6π)|= \\cos (-x - 2π \\cdot 3); = \\cos (-x)$.\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\cos{x}$, we have\n$\\cos (-x)|= \\cos x; = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos (-x-6\\pi)= \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "806c4106-fdbd-51d2-94a1-904d5dd58359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245979} {:answer/id #uuid "fe12d097-d886-52a8-bf6f-e71a29bdfa44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245980} {:answer/id #uuid "df9edfc5-a2d4-534b-876e-356707bf448d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245981} {:answer/id #uuid "9578e57f-9fa6-5d5f-aef3-0b4eaec2c230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245982} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1bb664-c324-55c9-b524-5c845ee46d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9578e57f-9fa6-5d5f-aef3-0b4eaec2c230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245982} :answer-field/id #uuid "1280a979-a21c-554b-ba9d-5f015b56b039" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245978}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ce20ec7-65da-5087-870d-4b3160d2bf4b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103230" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$\n\n- The function is zero at $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n- Its range is $y\\in [-2,2]$\n- $y=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is an output of the function" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933487/assets/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. At $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$, the function $y=cos⁡x$ takes the value zero.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. The range of the function $y=cos⁡x$ is $y\\in [-1,1].$ Thus, $y=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is not an output of this function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c0974d3-7ac3-57bf-8b93-a50e9c17e68d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245986} {:answer/id #uuid "b532cf81-39e3-54eb-8de2-40bfd93a2f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245987} {:answer/id #uuid "b8613a1a-201b-5386-906d-6bc752b9b3f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245988} {:answer/id #uuid "f32858ea-fcd8-5bfd-925a-aaf865669faf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245989} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4af29b-d76b-5265-9b5c-927bad3bf662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac4af29b-d76b-5265-9b5c-927bad3bf662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245990} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ed1293-632b-5a48-a28b-62a681f99d05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245985}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c77f0bbb-0038-59fb-98e0-040a634b36d9" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103233" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The zeros of the function are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi $\n- Its graph passes through the point $(0,1)$\n- The function has maximum values at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933487/assets/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The zeros occur at $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…,$ which can be written as $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is true. At $x=0,$ the function has the value $y=cos⁡0=1.$ Therefore, the graph of the function passes through the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a maximum at $x=0,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=2n\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c25c3a0-083a-55d5-b688-7347bfafaa9e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a4978a-022c-576d-9ee2-f2d4b4274c70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{8}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245993} {:answer/id #uuid "17e36097-4a15-5366-ba92-5c7486e0b255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245994} {:answer/id #uuid "d918cebd-83c4-5f6c-8f67-704dbb78a5f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245995} {:answer/id #uuid "1d08cec9-7b85-589f-9c2e-ce71a7136cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245996} {:answer/id #uuid "85694dc1-05b6-513f-8def-37bd4f062e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d918cebd-83c4-5f6c-8f67-704dbb78a5f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245995} :answer-field/id #uuid "df8e5566-e493-509d-a624-29ee2208e1ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245992}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01ff4749-78a9-5042-8697-916d5b164129" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103242" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=-\\frac{5}{8},$ then $cos⁡(x+12\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=6$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2\\cdot 6\\cdot \\pi )=cos⁡(x+12\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=-\\frac{5}{8},$ then $cos⁡(x+12\\pi )=-\\frac{5}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c25c3a0-083a-55d5-b688-7347bfafaa9e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a97b48d5-54ee-5c70-9f26-cff126ac4bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246000} {:answer/id #uuid "417f7eb7-9a25-524b-8474-a0d2cf0c5645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246001} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9f1612-2755-5cde-b713-d3227dbc358c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246002} {:answer/id #uuid "92b72e50-0ab3-579c-b5e5-94bc2d6d46ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246003} {:answer/id #uuid "51c05a39-e6aa-5d3d-8ba9-293193bf167d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f9f1612-2755-5cde-b713-d3227dbc358c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246002} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4cf07b6-e895-5693-879f-c4b885b0e954" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245999}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47f4cefb-503d-5938-a4aa-c4290ab39adc" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103243" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ then $cos⁡(x-14\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2\\cdot (-7)\\cdot \\pi )=cos⁡(x-14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ then $cos⁡(x-14\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c25c3a0-083a-55d5-b688-7347bfafaa9e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ff9b7c5-f997-5b95-bc86-36326207462a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246007} {:answer/id #uuid "cab815cc-2400-5788-8b63-dc4c51854c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246008} {:answer/id #uuid "639ffe51-3529-5bad-bf6a-91a03324fcac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246009} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae15af7-8e03-54d4-811c-bd33c90e5a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246010} {:answer/id #uuid "e991b10b-e069-5e3c-b84b-79f1576875ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cab815cc-2400-5788-8b63-dc4c51854c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246008} :answer-field/id #uuid "be4603d2-8fb3-59ec-acc4-7f1fbdf20930" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246006}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d77b4be-faf6-593f-b380-5ebb8440ba40" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103279" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=\\frac{3}{8},$ then $4cos⁡(x-10\\pi )+5=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2\\cdot (-5)\\cdot \\pi )=cos⁡(x-10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=\\frac{3}{8},$ then $cos⁡(x-10\\pi )=\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4cos⁡(x-10\\pi )+5 & =4(\\frac{3}{8})+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94645507-186a-54a7-a96b-ccfb0fb1f769" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e2c8de2-8e98-5c82-87a4-353110263110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174271} {:answer/id #uuid "e857146a-3e93-51a1-89a9-e74550fe2100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174272} {:answer/id #uuid "59f8ed8e-ae4f-509e-9a2d-3fdd6f146471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174273} {:answer/id #uuid "77607a6c-5be9-5b0a-9cf3-4d779a887700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174274} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9edf4b-963f-5298-bd0d-647e98728407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77607a6c-5be9-5b0a-9cf3-4d779a887700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174274} :answer-field/id #uuid "e15cc57f-a06e-5f3f-bc05-daabb0d923f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174270}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "195eeaaa-ad0f-5b04-bea4-14b7076ff8c6" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-136545" :question/problem "If $\\cos a =-\\frac{1}{3}$, then $\\cos (-a) =$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933487/assets/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-a) & =cos⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94645507-186a-54a7-a96b-ccfb0fb1f769" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5fbf963-ac32-5ca4-90ec-465e78236fc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246014} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e104ca-e69e-5127-a49b-b271fc874a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246015} {:answer/id #uuid "fcaf0e04-920b-570d-9bd7-238aab45b4ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{91}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246016} {:answer/id #uuid "23ba3bfc-3a9f-5549-bb2b-692205a8a171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246017} {:answer/id #uuid "0c63958c-e62d-5b68-be00-819f27cc558c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23ba3bfc-3a9f-5549-bb2b-692205a8a171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246017} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8ba5ade-2703-5903-a516-67e0b331d359" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246013}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eed134e6-6361-52f7-81cf-faaa10d7135c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-136629" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=-\\frac{3}{10},$ then $cos⁡(-x)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933487/assets/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94645507-186a-54a7-a96b-ccfb0fb1f769" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d55d76a-b8cc-559c-a314-c33f596f4581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246021} {:answer/id #uuid "7d763591-2ba3-5b26-b8ef-fff00a5b970a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246022} {:answer/id #uuid "b5631b90-69ed-5d94-a8db-b2cca6b607a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246023} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2e32a4-9793-50e7-8585-1afe0d907003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246024} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4666af-c562-5434-ad39-6e0ae7ed807b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5631b90-69ed-5d94-a8db-b2cca6b607a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246023} :answer-field/id #uuid "24ccc1ac-3ec4-55b5-b8e8-35271fa092f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246020}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eba6fd43-6b79-5c25-a91f-cc7289bc2939" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-158047" :question/problem "If $cos⁡a=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ then $cos⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933487/assets/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-a) & =cos⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94645507-186a-54a7-a96b-ccfb0fb1f769" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bc11836-201d-5dd7-ad48-634cd15e25f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246028} {:answer/id #uuid "548673d6-eb9e-50c5-a3d5-e815804e1ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246029} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4e6405-2d11-52ee-bb36-7e8b629a1506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246030} {:answer/id #uuid "9d88a5dd-06d8-55e9-8c38-d869237cf1e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246031} {:answer/id #uuid "8c8f3a7e-8912-5c89-b5de-b9117d5ebe35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c8f3a7e-8912-5c89-b5de-b9117d5ebe35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246032} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c54c152-b539-5b15-82c8-ad2bdb4ed8ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246027}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b27e325b-827a-5d71-8f63-a43865d31098" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-158052" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=-1,$ then $cos⁡(-x+24\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933487/assets/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x+24\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 12) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $cos⁡(-x+24\\pi )=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94645507-186a-54a7-a96b-ccfb0fb1f769" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb420bb-3d63-53ba-9980-bc74ae6cb5a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246035} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc323c8-b9be-5eb9-b6a6-5f1ce08e50e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246036} {:answer/id #uuid "73e4ee61-8b56-5633-a858-b76c9d0bced6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246037} {:answer/id #uuid "86d5b574-a6ee-51d9-95dd-60707d4c5b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246038} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff8e757-364d-519c-91f1-f3aa988ad5ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cb420bb-3d63-53ba-9980-bc74ae6cb5a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246035} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6a4f6c9-a608-5d09-af9c-db72e6bf71d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246034}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3a9e284-20ad-5ca4-9756-497c45717d6d" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-158059" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=0,$ then $\\pi cos⁡(-x+18\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933487/assets/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric over the $y$-axis. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x+18\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 9) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi cos⁡(-x+18\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pi \\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34290d39-66b3-5667-b17c-053670fde950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246042} {:answer/id #uuid "800e2914-1ae3-5695-aea8-6f2b93d9b8dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246043} {:answer/id #uuid "bffb5c34-7002-5350-9b21-4514c14f7f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246044} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d0f835-0075-5248-9c41-f63536849f08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246045} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea5f943-b873-578c-827f-14d63bdc8212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bffb5c34-7002-5350-9b21-4514c14f7f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246044} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba5d82ac-0ace-5ed9-8db1-a6b2d9512e01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246041}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe7dc7f0-1797-5ea1-82ae-3fc8dd0cfef1" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-27009" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$ \n\n- The function intersects the $x$-axis at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n- The function has a minimum value at  $x=2\\pi .$\n- The function intersects the $y$-axis at $y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933487/assets/9c77bec4ebb04668fbff5176418ed9c09143a8660879fdf523a158c5f109006c.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ the function intersects the $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has a maximum at $x=2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc4c251b-aebe-53c1-b5d6-4eb73fc0a201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246049} {:answer/id #uuid "48a5881f-1ef0-5fd2-bc52-2aafb1b96225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246050} {:answer/id #uuid "a267687e-58eb-59e7-a5c6-b443c6adc495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246051} {:answer/id #uuid "7f122c9a-5bdf-5d6d-8cd0-9befb2cff6df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246052} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fae0d0-3a6c-5972-9f42-618a98a872b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9fae0d0-3a6c-5972-9f42-618a98a872b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246053} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d3c8bff-afb2-59b8-b988-98dfec6ec33d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246048}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8200e68-ad7d-55ad-afa5-1d575a43e94f" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-70666" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The function has maximum values at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .$\n- The function is zero at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n- The range of the function is $[-\\pi ,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933487/assets/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has a maximum at $x=0,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=2n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is true. At $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, the function intersects the $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false. The minimum value is $-1$, the maximum value is $1$, and so the range is $[-1,1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6948a00-93d6-52dd-a25f-bc17e904f3d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246056} {:answer/id #uuid "f191aaae-cdc8-5f9e-8dd3-2f8192c75020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246057} {:answer/id #uuid "64b95b00-877f-55a0-ace4-a7033907017a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246058} {:answer/id #uuid "41302d26-6b7d-580d-a54d-4e85efb10779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246059} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e54ffe-5beb-52ac-929b-2d28492cdac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41302d26-6b7d-580d-a54d-4e85efb10779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246059} :answer-field/id #uuid "43e9852e-dcb2-5261-a502-12fb3c5c1c3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246055}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f94ac7f-8a62-5418-b585-93b6d31e2347" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-70667" :question/problem "Which of the following statements regarding the graph of $y=cos⁡x$ are true?\n\n- The graph intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1$\n- The function has a minimum value at $x=-\\pi .$\n- The range of the function is $[-\\pi ,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933487/assets/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The $y$-intercepts of the function is $y=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function at $x=-\\pi $ has a minimum value.\n\n- Statement III is false. The minimum value is $-1$, the maximum value is $1$, and so the range is $[-1,1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c25c3a0-083a-55d5-b688-7347bfafaa9e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba8538de-b3b9-59d5-842e-ad5dfd645baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246063} {:answer/id #uuid "dad96623-bcc7-5a98-9f2f-811b41e1f7e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246064} {:answer/id #uuid "bc831aad-60e0-5994-ada9-732cff603a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246065} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7b3c68-25d5-58bf-9fc2-e6b736ffc2e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246066} {:answer/id #uuid "55c3feb2-ae63-553e-b1d9-3ee48a2ef99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc831aad-60e0-5994-ada9-732cff603a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246065} :answer-field/id #uuid "de648d6f-c216-5525-9bfb-2a28332d8aec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246062}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af78f5cd-518a-50d2-99e1-f939086f6091" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-70671" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}$ for some $x$, then $cos⁡(x-10\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-5$ into the right-hand-side gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x-10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=\\frac{1}{2},$ then $cos⁡(x-10\\pi )=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c25c3a0-083a-55d5-b688-7347bfafaa9e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9c22e2d-70b4-5fe4-907e-6865ff4bed7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}+16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246070} {:answer/id #uuid "125ff7b4-5d0d-56fd-8856-30d9baf615eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246071} {:answer/id #uuid "d31939a9-c231-5eb8-a01f-63cce0f928c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246072} {:answer/id #uuid "8f517712-4c9b-5b6d-8a17-3fb539a2749c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}+16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246073} {:answer/id #uuid "f11d700b-13ed-52a0-a4bf-c644cc80a10c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d31939a9-c231-5eb8-a01f-63cce0f928c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246072} :answer-field/id #uuid "b63e8600-ae45-55b9-be5b-042a6d3f6e71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246069}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf29cf39-08af-5f38-b9a8-3d3454c76e3c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-70672" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for some $x$, then $cos⁡(x+16\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=8$ into the right-hand-side gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x+16\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ then $cos⁡(x+16\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$"}]}
